Create a production-ready  build of a custom HyperNix T1 API that is easy to integrate into any server and can be consumed by the HyperNix TUI (make a new tui called waiter that uses t1 api , and t1 api keys and has a single command auto setup and configure using waiter serv -A (auto configure) -I “ip address or Tailscale ip or public/localhost url” -K “convert normal t1 token into admin token and token here” -E (encrypt), (optional) -B (blacklist ip’s) -L (local Tailscale and local host only), -P (port), -W (whitist), -r (force limit on specific t1 key’s server id), -a (appeal ip’s from blacklist) and -h (help), -C (other settings), and -H “home page url) lastly -G (open tui) -g (open cli)) -R (quick refresh) -Rf (force refresh full) -y (sync default configuration or server config) as a first-class backend.  
Project goal: Build a secure, modular HTTP API wrapper around HyperNix-pip version 0.71.5b1 that lets an admin T1 key control model/module operations, usage reporting, and remote/local server deployment. The API must be easy to mount into an existing Python server, expose clean OpenAPI docs, and be simple to call from a TUI client.  
Hard requirements:  
* Use a clean, server-friendly architecture with FastAPI or an equivalent modern framework.  
* Support admin-authenticated T1 API keys and user-scoped tokens.  
* Never log raw API keys or tokens.  
* Provide middleware for auth, rate limiting, request tracing, and audit logs.  
* Expose clear JSON responses with stable schemas and good error messages.  
* Include OpenAPI/Swagger docs and a minimal SDK-friendly response format.  
* Make the system work both for local-only deployments and remote multi-server deployments.  
* Integrate directly with HyperNix-pip so the server can use its upload, train, chat, quantize, convert, and TUI-adjacent capabilities.  
* Support module creation, module upload, module sync, module versioning, module listing, and remote deployment from local files.  
* Support retrieving remaining usage, current usage, historical usage, and estimated cost of spent usage.  
* Support admin-only key management and server configuration.  
* Make it easy for hyped tui to discover and call the API.  
Architecture expectations:  
* Separate auth, usage metering, module registry, server registry, and job execution into distinct services or routers.  
* Support environment-variable configuration plus optional .env loading.  
* Use Pydantic models for request/response validation.  
* Persist data in a lightweight database such as SQLite for dev and PostgreSQL for production.  
* Support encrypted secret storage for tokens/keys at rest.  
* Provide background jobs for uploads, sync, and remote deployment.  
* Include a plugin-like extension system for future endpoints.  
Primary endpoint set:  
1. GET /health — health check, version, uptime.  
2. GET /status — service status, active modules, queue depth.  
3. POST /auth/t1/validate — validate a T1 admin key.  
4. POST /auth/t1/admin/rotate — rotate admin key.  
5. POST /auth/t1/rotate — rotate normal general key  
6. POST /auth/token — mint a scoped access token.  
7. GET /usage/remaining — remaining usage quota.  
8. GET /usage/current — current usage snapshot.  
9. GET /usage/history — usage history by date/range.  
10. GET /usage/cost — cost summary for spent usage.  
11. GET /usage/estimate — estimate cost before a job runs.  
12. GET /modules — list modules.  
13. POST /modules/create — create a module record.  
14. POST /modules/upload/local — upload a module from a local file.  
15. POST /modules/upload/remote — pull a module from a non-local server.  
16. GET /modules/{module_id} — module details.  
17. PATCH /modules/{module_id} — update module metadata/version.  
18. DELETE /modules/{module_id} — remove a module.  
19. POST /modules/{module_id}/sync — sync module to configured targets.  
20. GET /servers — list configured servers.  
21. POST /servers/register — register a local or remote server.  
22. PATCH /servers/{server_id} — update server settings.  
23. DELETE /servers/{server_id} — remove server.  
24. GET /keys — list key metadata without revealing secrets.  
25. POST /keys/import — import a key securely.  
26. POST /keys/assign — assign a key to a server or module scope.  
27. POST /jobs — submit an async job.  
28. GET /jobs/{job_id} — job progress and result.  
29. POST /jobs/{job_id}/cancel — cancel a running job.  
30. GET /events — stream events for TUI/live updates.  
31. GET /config — safe read-only config summary.  
Behavior rules:  
* Admin T1 keys can create, upload, deploy, sync, rotate, and delete.  
* Non-admin tokens can only read allowed module/usage data and call models there is also a payment token for adding balance to accounts or servers  
* All write actions must be idempotent where possible.  
* Return a unique request ID in every response.  
* Every job must have status states like queued, running, succeeded, failed, cancelled.  
* Usage endpoints must support per-key, per-server, and per-module breakdowns.  
* Cost endpoints must support totals, forecasts, and date ranges.  
* Upload endpoints must accept local paths, file uploads, and remote URLs when permitted.  
* Remote uploads must verify target server trust before execution.  
* The API must expose clean, stable names that the TUI can call directly.  
HyperNix integration requirements:  
* Wrap the HyperNix-pip backend as a service layer for the api package  
* Use its existing model/module workflow rather than duplicating model logic.  
* Expose helper functions for conversion, quantization, training, chat, and upload if present in the backend.  
* Make the endpoint names and payloads consistent with a TUI client that needs fast discovery and minimal friction.  
* Include adapter code for hyped tui so it can:    
    * authenticate with a T1 key,  
    * list modules,  
    * upload a local module,  
    * push to remote servers,  
    * show remaining usage,  
    * show spent cost  
    * watch live job progress.  
Security requirements:  
* Store secrets encrypted.  
* Hash API keys or store only a keyed digest for lookup.  
* Use least-privilege scopes.  
* Add IP allowlists and optional mTLS support.  
* Add audit logs for all admin actions.  
* Sanitize file paths to prevent directory traversal.  
* Validate remote URLs before fetching anything.  
* Require explicit confirmation for destructive operations.  
* Complete encryption of all keys fully end to end  
Implementation deliverables:  
* Full API server code.  
* OpenAPI schema.  
* Example .env.  
* Minimal TUI integration adapter.  
* Example requests/responses for every endpoint.  
* Documentation with setup, auth flow, and HyperNix integration notes.  
* A small CLI for smoke testing.  
* Tests for auth, usage, module upload,  
  
  
